Natural language processing allows machines to read and understand human language Many different problems in machine translation speech recognition text classification and others have been solved to varying degrees Specific problems include the ability to determine whether a string of characters is grammatically valid determining what a string of characters means translating text between languages and generating text Text can come from a wide variety of sources including typed input speech recognition optical character recognition and other sources Machine translation has been studied since the nineteen fifties The Georgetown experiment in nineteen fifty four involved fully automatic translation of more than sixty Russian sentences into English The authors claimed that within three or five years machine translation would be a solved problem however real progress was much slower

The field of computer vision focuses on how computers can gain high level understanding from digital images or videos From the perspective of engineering computer vision seeks to understand and automate tasks that the human visual system can do Computer vision tasks include methods for acquiring processing analyzing and understanding digital images and extraction of high dimensional data from the real world in order to produce numerical or symbolic information Image understanding can be seen as the disentangling of symbolic information from image data using models constructed with the aid of geometry physics statistics and learning theory


The history of artificial intelligence began in antiquity with myths stories and rumors of artificial beings endowed with intelligence or consciousness by master craftsmen As Pamela McCorduck wrote machines who think have been the focus of popular imagination since ancient times and the formalization of the idea for a discipline began in the early twentieth century when the British mathematician Alan Turing proposed what is now called the Turing test as a criterion of intelligence In the paper Computing Machinery and Intelligence published in the journal Mind Turing proposed that rather than asking whether machines can think we should ask whether machines can pass a behavioral test Turing proposed that a human evaluator would judge natural language conversations between a human and a machine designed to generate human like responses The evaluator would know that one of the two partners in conversation is a machine and all participants would be separated from one another The conversation would be limited to a text only channel such as a computer keyboard and screen so the result would not depend on the ability to render words as speech If the evaluator could not reliably tell the machine from the human the machine would be said to have passed the test The test does not check the ability to give correct answers to questions only how closely answers resemble those a human would give

The field of artificial intelligence research was founded at a workshop held on the campus of Dartmouth College during the summer of nineteen fifty six Those who attended would become the leaders of AI research for decades Their programs amazed computers at chess solving word problems proving logical theorems and speaking English By the middle of the nineteen sixties research in the US was heavily funded by the Department of Defense and laboratories had been established around the world Artificial intelligence founders were optimistic about the future Herbert Simon predicted that machines will be capable within twenty years of doing any work a man can do Marvin Minsky agreed writing that within a generation the problem of creating artificial intelligence will substantially be solved

They failed to recognize the difficulty of some of the remaining tasks In nineteen seventy four in response to the criticism of Sir James Lighthill and ongoing pressure from the US Congress to fund more productive projects both the US and British governments cut off exploratory research in AI The next few years would later be called an AI winter a period when obtaining funding for AI projects was difficult During the nineteen eighties a form of AI program called expert systems was adopted by corporations around the world and knowledge became the focus of mainstream AI research The Japanese government aggressively funded AI with its fifth generation computer project In the early nineteen nineties Japanese funding was abandoned after the goals were not met and once again AI fell into disrepute and a second AI winter began

